From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winex11, explorer: a framed window chosen or clicked comes
 in front

Activation does not raise a window in Wine's Z order by itself; on the
installed system (Xwayland) the X window could come in front while the
Win32 order stayed as it was. So:

- a click into a Linux program's window made its frame active but left it
  under the window it was clicked over (Firefox under Notepad); the frame
  is raised first, as a window clicked is;
- the window chosen with Alt+Tab or in Task View stayed under a frame in
  Wine's order, though drawn in front, and the next Alt+Tab, which goes by
  that order, chose it again (David 2026-10-02: "releasing Alt+Tab on a
  window does not select it"); it is raised, as Windows' switchers bring
  it.

Verified on the QA VM (click into Firefox, then Alt+Tab four times:
Notepad, Firefox, Notepad, Firefox). Xvfb keeps Wine's order in step
without these raises, so linuxembed-gate.sh, which now also checks Wine's
Z order after the click and each Alt+Tab, does not catch their mutants
(EMBED_CLICK_NO_RAISE, SWITCH_NO_RAISE).
---
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/sg_embed.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/sg_embed.c
@@ -545,6 +545,13 @@
     switch (msg)
     {
     case WM_X11DRV_SG_EMBED_ACTIVATE:
+        /* a click into the program: its frame comes in front, as a window
+         * clicked does -- Wine saw no click on it (the press went on to the
+         * program), and it was made active but left under the window it was
+         * clicked over (David 2026-10-02, Firefox under Notepad) */
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_EMBED_CLICK_NO_RAISE
+        NtUserSetWindowPos( hwnd, HWND_TOP, 0, 0, 0, 0, SWP_NOMOVE | SWP_NOSIZE | SWP_NOACTIVATE );
+#endif
         NtUserSetForegroundWindow( hwnd );
         if ((data = get_win_data( hwnd )))
         {
--- a/programs/explorer/vdesktop.c
+++ b/programs/explorer/vdesktop.c
@@ -1119,6 +1119,19 @@
     if (cmd) tv_refresh( hwnd );
 }
 
+/* The window chosen (Alt+Tab, Task View) comes to the top, as Windows'
+ * switchers bring it: activation alone does not raise it in Wine's Z order
+ * (the X window may be in front, the Win32 order unchanged) -- a window
+ * chosen stayed under a Linux program's frame there, and the next Alt+Tab
+ * chose it again (David 2026-10-02: "releasing Alt+Tab on a window does
+ * not select it"). */
+static void switch_raise( HWND target )
+{
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_SWITCH_NO_RAISE
+    SetWindowPos( target, HWND_TOP, 0, 0, 0, 0, SWP_NOMOVE | SWP_NOSIZE | SWP_NOACTIVATE );
+#endif
+}
+
 static void tv_activate( int i )
 {
     HWND target = tv.items[i].hwnd;
@@ -1139,6 +1152,7 @@
     }
     /* active before Task View goes, as from the switcher (switcher_commit) */
     if (IsIconic( target )) ShowWindow( target, SW_RESTORE );
+    switch_raise( target );
     SetForegroundWindow( target );
     tv_close();
 }
@@ -1387,8 +1401,8 @@
         if (target) sg_linux_window_command( target, L"XACTIVATE" );
         return;
     }
-    /* the window chosen is active (and so in front) before the switcher
-     * goes: the switcher gone while it was this thread's active window, Wine
+    /* the window chosen is in front and active before the switcher goes:
+     * the switcher gone while it was this thread's active window, Wine
      * activated the thread's window before it -- a Linux program's frame,
      * which is explorer's -- and it came back above the window chosen; the
      * next Alt+Tab chose the same window again (David 2026-10-02) */
@@ -1396,6 +1410,7 @@
     DestroyWindow( hwnd );
 #endif
     if (IsIconic( target )) ShowWindow( target, SW_RESTORE );
+    switch_raise( target );
     SetForegroundWindow( target );
     DestroyWindow( hwnd );
 }
